{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015vpMezWeb9H2bhASZiS9zN","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tPAYLOAD 25/32. One file changed: .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh (2 hunks). I read the candidate file in full. I also read the restore side it pairs with: .claude/scripts/ceo-restore.sh:230-340 and .claude/scripts/_restore_archive.py. I checked the CHANGELOG [1.4.4] section \"Fixed — backup and restore\" (CHANGELOG.md:274-298) and the new test .claude/scripts/tests/test_ceo_restore.py:185-202 (test_fu144_backup_does_not_follow_memory_link).\n2\t\n3\tWHAT THE DELTA DOES\n4\t- Hunk 1 (ceo-backup.sh:247-256): the old line `cp -pR \"$AUDIT_DIR/memory/.\" ...` copied links as links. It is replaced by a loop over `find \"$AUDIT_DIR/memory\" -type f -print0`, using `read -r -d ''`, `mkdir -p` of the parent directory and `cp -p --`. Only regular files reach the staging directory. This matches the restore validator added in this train: it refuses any member that is not a file or directory (_restore_archive.py:112-113), and ceo-restore.sh:235 refuses any link in the staging directory. Under the old code, a single link in memory/ produced an archive that restore refused as a whole. The fix is correct in direction.\n5\t- Hunk 2 (ceo-backup.sh:276-280): `COPYFILE_DISABLE=1` is set for tar, inside the subshell only. Apple's bsdtar honours it and then writes no `._<name>` AppleDouble members. GNU tar ignores it. The comment and the CHANGELOG lines 286-289 are accurate.\n6\t\n7\tCHECKS AGAINST THE REVIEW AXES\n8\t- Adopter blast radius: this is a manual or cron backup script. It touches no hook, governance setting, model or effort, and it cannot block a session.\n9\t- Fail direction: not a hook; no inversion of the ADR-186 or hook fail-open rules.\n10\t- Writes: only to the mktemp staging directory and to $BACKUP_DIR, as before. The new loop never follows a link in memory/. find runs with its default -P, and `-type f` leaves out links to files.\n11\t- Portability: the script is bash (#!/bin/bash). Process substitution and `read -d ''` work in bash 3.2 on macOS, and the same construct is already used at line 301. The quoted prefix strip `${f#\"$AUDIT_DIR/memory/\"}` is literal. It stays correct for a relative --audit-dir and for a trailing slash (`//memory/` matches what find prints).\n12\t- Moved code / Python: no Python and no re-exports in this payload.\n13\t- Honesty: CHANGELOG.md:276-277 (\"archives only the regular files of memory/; a link there is left out\") and :289 (\"no longer writes these members\") match the code.\n14\t- Declared conditions: none of conditions 1-14 makes a claim this payload contradicts. The file is in scope (condition 13), and the CHANGELOG lines that cover it are true.\n15\t\n16\tP0: none.\n17\t\n18\tNEW FINDINGS (annex): none at P1.\n19\t\n20\tP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n21\t1. P2, .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh:264, together with _restore_archive.py:112-113 and ceo-restore.sh:235. This hunk stops copying links for memory/ only. The `--include-plans` branch still runs `cp -pR \"$ADOPTER_ROOT/.claude/plans/.\" ...`, which keeps links. Restore now refuses every link member, which is new in [1.4.4].\n22\t   Failure scenario: an adopter runs `ceo-backup.sh --include-plans` and `.claude/plans/` contains one link. The backup prints \"ok\" and exits 0, but ceo-restore.sh refuses the whole archive (\"refused member type\"), including audit/ and memory/. Manual `tar -xzf` still recovers the data.\n23\t   Minimal fix: copy plans/ with the same loop (`find ... -type f -print0` plus `cp -p --`).\n24\t2. P2, .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh:247 and :255.\n25\t   Failure scenario: `$AUDIT_DIR/memory` is itself a link to a directory, for example a synced memory folder. `[ -d ]` is true, but find with its default -P does not follow a link given on the command line, so it lists nothing. The archive gets an empty memory/, the MANIFEST lists no memory file, and the run exits 0 with no warning. The base version followed `memory/.` and backed the files up. The CHANGELOG wording roughly covers this case, so this is a silent loss of coverage rather than a false claim.\n26\t   Minimal fix: print a warning to stderr when `[ -L \"$AUDIT_DIR/memory\" ]`, or use `find -H` for that starting point only. The archive would still hold only regular files.\n27\t3. P2, .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh:255. If find hits an unreadable subdirectory inside memory/, it exits non-zero, but bash ignores the exit status of a process substitution. The backup is then partial, exits 0, and only find's own stderr line reports it. The old `cp -pR` failed under set -e.\n28\t   Minimal fix: read find's status after the loop, or run find into a temporary list first and check its exit code.\n29\t4. P2, .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh:253. `dirname \"$rel\"` without `--` fails on a name that starts with \"-\".\n30\t   Effect for a top-level file: only an error message on stderr.\n31\t   Effect for a subdirectory such as `-x/y.md`: mkdir does not create `-x`, the next cp fails, and set -e aborts the backup. It fails closed and loudly.\n32\t   Minimal fix: `dirname -- \"$rel\"`. The same line exists at ceo-restore.sh:322.\n33\t\n34\tVERDICT: GO This payload holds only the ceo-backup.sh change, which correctly limits the memory/ snapshot to regular files and stops macOS tar from writing AppleDouble members, as the CHANGELOG states; it falsifies no declared condition and contains no P0 or undeclared P1, only four P2 edge cases.\n35\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"554a7565-eb62-4598-9760-aeebd94af377","uuid":"d9200f83-c1a9-4356-a300-f96c8c799c00","timestamp":"2026-11-16T19:42:46.629Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.n5R3O9/tree/.claude/plans/PLAN-198/repass-rc1/claude-rc1-25.verdict.txt","content":"PAYLOAD 25/32. One file changed: .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh (2 hunks). I read the candidate file in full. I also read the restore side it pairs with: .claude/scripts/ceo-restore.sh:230-340 and .claude/scripts/_restore_archive.py. I checked the CHANGELOG [1.4.4] section \"Fixed — backup and restore\" (CHANGELOG.md:274-298) and the new test .claude/scripts/tests/test_ceo_restore.py:185-202 (test_fu144_backup_does_not_follow_memory_link).\n\nWHAT THE DELTA DOES\n- Hunk 1 (ceo-backup.sh:247-256): the old line `cp -pR \"$AUDIT_DIR/memory/.\" ...` copied links as links. It is replaced by a loop over `find \"$AUDIT_DIR/memory\" -type f -print0`, using `read -r -d ''`, `mkdir -p` of the parent directory and `cp -p --`. Only regular files reach the staging directory. This matches the restore validator added in this train: it refuses any member that is not a file or directory (_restore_archive.py:112-113), and ceo-restore.sh:235 refuses any link in the staging directory. Under the old code, a single link in memory/ produced an archive that restore refused as a whole. The fix is correct in direction.\n- Hunk 2 (ceo-backup.sh:276-280): `COPYFILE_DISABLE=1` is set for tar, inside the subshell only. Apple's bsdtar honours it and then writes no `._<name>` AppleDouble members. GNU tar ignores it. The comment and the CHANGELOG lines 286-289 are accurate.\n\nCHECKS AGAINST THE REVIEW AXES\n- Adopter blast radius: this is a manual or cron backup script. It touches no hook, governance setting, model or effort, and it cannot block a session.\n- Fail direction: not a hook; no inversion of the ADR-186 or hook fail-open rules.\n- Writes: only to the mktemp staging directory and to $BACKUP_DIR, as before. The new loop never follows a link in memory/. find runs with its default -P, and `-type f` leaves out links to files.\n- Portability: the script is bash (#!/bin/bash). Process substitution and `read -d ''` work in bash 3.2 on macOS, and the same construct is already used at line 301. The quoted prefix strip `${f#\"$AUDIT_DIR/memory/\"}` is literal. It stays correct for a relative --audit-dir and for a trailing slash (`//memory/` matches what find prints).\n- Moved code / Python: no Python and no re-exports in this payload.\n- Honesty: CHANGELOG.md:276-277 (\"archives only the regular files of memory/; a link there is left out\") and :289 (\"no longer writes these members\") match the code.\n- Declared conditions: none of conditions 1-14 makes a claim this payload contradicts. The file is in scope (condition 13), and the CHANGELOG lines that cover it are true.\n\nP0: none.\n\nNEW FINDINGS (annex): none at P1.\n\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n1. P2, .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh:264, together with _restore_archive.py:112-113 and ceo-restore.sh:235. This hunk stops copying links for memory/ only. The `--include-plans` branch still runs `cp -pR \"$ADOPTER_ROOT/.claude/plans/.\" ...`, which keeps links. Restore now refuses every link member, which is new in [1.4.4].\n   Failure scenario: an adopter runs `ceo-backup.sh --include-plans` and `.claude/plans/` contains one link. The backup prints \"ok\" and exits 0, but ceo-restore.sh refuses the whole archive (\"refused member type\"), including audit/ and memory/. Manual `tar -xzf` still recovers the data.\n   Minimal fix: copy plans/ with the same loop (`find ... -type f -print0` plus `cp -p --`).\n2. P2, .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh:247 and :255.\n   Failure scenario: `$AUDIT_DIR/memory` is itself a link to a directory, for example a synced memory folder. `[ -d ]` is true, but find with its default -P does not follow a link given on the command line, so it lists nothing. The archive gets an empty memory/, the MANIFEST lists no memory file, and the run exits 0 with no warning. The base version followed `memory/.` and backed the files up. The CHANGELOG wording roughly covers this case, so this is a silent loss of coverage rather than a false claim.\n   Minimal fix: print a warning to stderr when `[ -L \"$AUDIT_DIR/memory\" ]`, or use `find -H` for that starting point only. The archive would still hold only regular files.\n3. P2, .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh:255. If find hits an unreadable subdirectory inside memory/, it exits non-zero, but bash ignores the exit status of a process substitution. The backup is then partial, exits 0, and only find's own stderr line reports it. The old `cp -pR` failed under set -e.\n   Minimal fix: read find's status after the loop, or run find into a temporary list first and check its exit code.\n4. P2, .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh:253. `dirname \"$rel\"` without `--` fails on a name that starts with \"-\".\n   Effect for a top-level file: only an error message on stderr.\n   Effect for a subdirectory such as `-x/y.md`: mkdir does not create `-x`, the next cp fails, and set -e aborts the backup. It fails closed and loudly.\n   Minimal fix: `dirname -- \"$rel\"`. The same line exists at ceo-restore.sh:322.\n\nVERDICT: GO This payload holds only the ceo-backup.sh change, which correctly limits the memory/ snapshot to regular files and stops macOS tar from writing AppleDouble members, as the CHANGELOG states; it falsifies no declared condition and contains no P0 or undeclared P1, only four P2 edge cases.\n","numLines":35,"startLine":1,"totalLines":35}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015vpMezWeb9H2bhASZiS9zN","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
