{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7BJpbS8XzPRWZH1fuqj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I found no P0 and no new P1 in payload 26, and none of the declared conditions is false against these files. I recorded the decision as GO.\n\n**What the payload holds**\n- `budget-summary.py` becomes a thin entry point over six new modules in `.claude/hooks/_lib/_budget_summary_parts/`. I read all six.\n- `caller_repo_guard.py` is new.\n- `ceo-backup.sh` changes in two places.\n\n**Conditions this payload touches**\n- **Condition 7 holds.** `budget-summary.py` keeps its path and command line, and its logic moves into `_lib` modules.\n- **Condition 5 is unaffected.** The new modules are not on the kernel list, which is correct: `budget-summary.py` is a command-line tool, and no registered hook imports it.\n- **The CHANGELOG lines are true.** Lines 276-277 and 286-289 (backup and macOS tar) and 342-346 (`caller_repo_guard.py` reaches installs) match the code.\n\n**Moved code**\n- All 40 removed functions reappear exactly once across the six modules.\n- One function, `_load_normalize_model_name`, exists twice: in `cost_rollup.py` and again in the entry point. The entry point's copy is defined later, so it wins, and the body is the same.\n- Every name the modules use exists in the entry point, which still has all its original imports.\n- The modules load on their own, so a module that fails to import cannot leave the tool half-built.\n- `__file__` lookups resolve to `.claude/scripts/`, so both helper files the tool loads are still found.\n- The values that depend on loading those helpers are set again at lines 425-429, before the argument parser is built.\n- The new modules directory has no `__init__.py`. It still imports on Python 3.9, and `install.sh:1524-1541` ships it with the rest of `_lib`.\n- Everything stays standard-library only and Python 3.9 compatible.\n\n**`caller_repo_guard.py`**\n- Its git calls only read, and it exits 2 if git fails, which is the safe direction for a checker.\n- On installs it does nothing unless someone runs it. Its only write is to the `--out` path you pass.\n\n**`ceo-backup.sh`**\n- The change works as described: the memory snapshot keeps only regular files, and macOS `tar` no longer writes `._` members.\n- Every gap I found in it was already reported in the rc review, and the code has not changed since the rc.\n\n**New findings (annex):** none at P1.\n\n**P2 follow-ups**\n1. `ceo-backup.sh:247,255`: if `memory` is itself a link to a folder, `find` lists nothing. The backup gets an empty `memory/` and exits 0 with no warning; before this change those files were backed up. Fix: use `find -H` for that folder, or print a warning. Already raised in the rc review.\n2. `ceo-backup.sh:255`: nothing checks whether `find` succeeded. An unreadable subfolder gives a partial backup that is checksummed and reported as OK; the old `cp -pR` stopped with an error. Fix: build the file list first, check `find`'s exit code, and stop before writing the archive. Already raised in the rc review.\n3. `ceo-backup.sh:253`: `dirname \"$rel\"` has no `--`, so a folder whose name starts with `-` makes the backup stop. Fix: `dirname -- \"$rel\"`; `ceo-restore.sh:322` has the same line.\n4. `ceo-backup.sh:264`: `--include-plans` still copies links, so restore refuses the whole archive. This was already filed as a P1 in the rc notes and is not new here.\n5. `budget-summary.py:371-378`: if an upgrade leaves out the new modules folder, the tool crashes on start with a traceback. It is not a hook and the failure is loud. Fix: catch the import error and print \"run upgrade.sh\".\n6. `budget-summary.py:400-422` against `cost_rollup.py:97-129`: there are two copies of `_load_normalize_model_name` to keep in sync. Fix: delete one.\n7. In the new modules folder, the modules import none of the names they use. Calling one of their functions directly fails, linters report undefined names, and reading a function's source shows the module file instead of `budget-summary.py`. I found no shipped code that relies on that. Fix: add a note at the top of each module, or the imports.\n8. `caller_repo_guard.py:51-53`: it clears only `GIT_DIR` and `GIT_WORK_TREE`. `GIT_COMMON_DIR`, `GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY`, `GIT_INDEX_FILE` and `GIT_NAMESPACE` still pass through and can point it at the wrong repository's refs. Fix: drop every name `git rev-parse --local-env-vars` prints."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2189,"cache_read_input_tokens":157514,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2189},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"554a7565-eb62-4598-9760-aeebd94af377","uuid":"39b3913d-7bee-4e36-8fab-2df144a7d933","timestamp":"2026-11-16T19:43:49.175Z","request_id":"req_011Ch7BJpNY1i5hR3yBa2bVU"}
