oint and all six part modules (audit_events, cost_rollup, benchmark_native, native_reconciliation, presentation_cli, native_formatting), the whole candidate ceo-backup.sh, install.sh:1480-1613, env-inventory.json, CHANGELOG.md:274-298 and :338-346, and the rc1 verdicts for the matching rc payloads (claude/codex rc1-24 and rc1-25). The GA-only condition says the code equals the rc, and nothing here contradicts that.\\n\\nDECLARED CONDITIONS THIS PAYLOAD TOUCHES\\n- Condition 7: TRUE. budget-summary.py keeps its path, CLI and entry point (main at :431-432), and its logic now lives in _lib/_budget_summary_parts/. A hand patch must be redone in the module, as the condition says.\\n- Condition 5: not affected. _budget_summary_parts is not on the kernel list, which is correct: budget-summary is a CLI, not a governance hook, and no registered hook imports it (a grep of .claude/hooks outside tests finds only comments).\\n- Conditions 13 and 14: all three paths and the six part modules are in scope (scope-rc1.txt:77-82), and none of them is withheld.\\n- CHANGELOG honesty: :276-277 (\\\"archives only the regular files of memory/; a link there is left out\\\"), :286-289 (no more AppleDouble members) and :342-346 (install copies every top-level .claude/scripts/*.py, so caller_repo_guard.py reaches installs; confirmed at install.sh:1606-1611) all match the code.\\n\\nMOVED CODE (budget-summary)\\n- All 40 removed functions reappear exactly once across the six parts, with no name collision. The one exception: _load_normalize_model_name exists in cost_rollup.py:97-129, is bound by the loop, and is then redefined in the entry point at :400-422. The later definition wins and has the same body; the entry point imports importlib.util at :69.\\n- The rebinding loop at :380-397 rebuilds each function over the entry point's globals() and keeps defaults, kwdefaults, annotations, qualname and __module__. Calls between functions and monkeypatched names (_NORMALIZE_MODEL_NAME, load_transcripts_instrument, the pricing tables) therefore resolve as before.\\n- Every free name the parts use exists in the entry point's globals: json, os, re, sys, glob, hashlib, argparse, importlib, datetime/timedelta/timezone, Path, _rp, and all the module constants (_SINCE_RE ... _AUDIT_ENV_CARRIERS, AUDIT_BANNER, SOURCE_CHOICES, ROOT_ENV, MEMORY_CLAIM_*).\\n- The parts import on their own. They only define functions, with string annotations from `from __future__ import annotations`. The only defaults that read a name (validate_memory_claim) are backed by native_reconciliation.py:3-6, whose values equal the entry point's :102-105.\\n- __file__ in load_transcripts_instrument and in _load_normalize_model_name resolves to .claude/scripts/, so ceo-cost-transcripts.py and optimizer/model_normalize.py are still found.\\n- _NORMALIZE_MODEL_NAME, _TX_MODULE, SOURCE_CHOICES, AUDIT_BANNER and ROOT_ENV are set again at :425-429, before build_parser can run.\\n- _budget_summary_parts has no __init__.py. It is a namespace subpackage under the regular _lib package, which works on Python 3.3+, and install_lib_selective (install.sh:1524-1541) ships every top-level _lib entry.\\n- Python 3.9: FunctionType(code, globals, name, argdefs, closure) is valid. Standard library only.\\n\\ncaller_repo_guard.py: its git calls only read; any git failure or an unreadable snapshot exits 2, which is fail-closed for a checker. It needs Python 3.7+ for subparsers(required=True). Standard library only. On installs it is inert unless run, and its only write is to the --out path the operator passes.\\n\\nceo-backup.sh: the memory/ loop is correct in direction and matches the restore validator. COPYFILE_DISABLE=1 applies only inside the tar subshell. It is not a hook, so it cannot block a session and touches no governance setting, model or effort. Its writes stay in the mktemp staging dir and $BACKUP_DIR.\\n\\nP0: none.\\n\\nNEW FINDINGS (annex): none at P1.\\n\\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\\n1. P2 - .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh:247 and :255. If $AUDIT_DIR/memory is itself a link to a directory, [ -d ] is true, but find with its default -P does not follow a link named on the command line, so it lists nothing. The archive gets an empty memory/ and the run exits 0 with no warning; the base version followed memory/. and backed the files up. Already raised as P2 #2 in rc1 claude-25 and unchanged in the GA. Minimal fix: `find -H \\\"$AUDIT_DIR/memory\\\" -type f -print0`, or a stderr warning when [ -L \\\"$AUDIT_DIR/memory\\\" ].\\n2. P2 - ceo-backup.sh:255. Bash ignores the exit status of a process substitution. An unreadable subdirectory gives a partial snapshot that is archived, checksummed, reported \\\"ok\\\" and entered into rotation; the old cp -pR aborted under set -e. Already raised in rc1 codex-25 and claude-25. Minimal fix: write find's NUL-delimited list to a temp file, check find's exit code, and abort before tar and rotation.\\n3. P2 - ceo-backup.sh:253. `dirname \\\"$rel\\\"` has no `--`. A subdirectory whose name starts with \\\"-\\\" makes dirname fail, mkdir skips the directory, and the next cp aborts the backup (loud, exit 1, not the documented 2). Minimal fix: `dirname -- \\\"$rel\\\"`; ceo-restore.sh:322 has the same line.\\n4. Carried, not new: ceo-backup.sh:264. The --include-plans branch still copies links with cp -pR, so ceo-restore.sh refuses the whole archive. It is already declared as a P1 in the rc lane notes (claude-p27) and stays open at GA.\\n5. P2 - budget-summary.py:371-378. The import of _lib._budget_summary_parts has no guard. If a partial upgrade leaves out that directory, the CLI dies at import with a traceback instead of a named error. It is loud and not a hook. Minimal fix: catch ImportError and print \\\"run scripts/upgrade.sh\\\" with exit 2.\\n6. P2 - budget-summary.py:400-422 against _lib/_budget_summary_parts/cost_rollup.py:97-129. Two copies of _load_normalize_model_name must be kept in sync; the entry point's copy wins and has lost the docstring. Minimal fix: delete one copy.\\n7. P2 - _lib/_budget_summary_parts/*.py. The part modules import none of the names they use (audit_events.py has no json, os, glob or hashlib import, for example), so calling a part's own function raises NameError, and pyflakes-style linting reports undefined names. inspect.getsource and co_filename of the entry point's functions now point at the part files. I found no in-scope consumer of either. Minimal fix: a module docstring stating that the functions only run after the entry point rebinds them, or explicit imports.\\n8. P2 - .claude/scripts/caller_repo_guard.py:51-53. The environment scrub removes only GIT_DIR and GIT_WORK_TREE. GIT_COMMON_DIR, GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY, GIT_INDEX_FILE and GIT_NAMESPACE still pass through, so a harness started from inside a git hook could fingerprint the wrong refs; the comment promises no variable that redirects the repository decides the answer. Already raised in rc1 claude-24. Minimal fix: drop every variable that `git rev-parse --local-env-vars` lists.\",\"decision\":\"GO\",\"sentence\":\"Payload 26 makes no declared condition false and has no P0 or new P1: the budget-summary split keeps its names, globals, patch points and file-relative paths on Python 3.9 with only the standard library, caller_repo_guard.py fails closed and only reads, and the ceo-backup.sh change does what the CHANGELOG says, leaving only P2 follow-ups that the rc review had mostly already recorded.\"}","structured_output":{"report":"PAYLOAD 26/34. Files changed: .claude/scripts/budget-summary.py (now a thin entry point over the new .claude/hooks/_lib/_budget_summary_parts/*), .claude/scripts/caller_repo_guard.py (new) and .claude/scripts/ceo-backup.sh (two hunks). I read the candidate entry point and all six part modules (audit_events, cost_rollup, benchmark_native, native_reconciliation, presentation_cli, native_formatting), the whole candidate ceo-backup.sh, install.sh:1480-1613, env-inventory.json, CHANGELOG.md:274-2